GNU grep for OpenVMS
====================

This is GNU grep @VERSION@ built natively for OpenVMS @ARCH@, from the
unmodified GNU release plus the patches in the vms-grep project:

    https://github.com/issinoho/vms-grep

Kit version: @KITVERSION@.  GNU grep is free software under the GNU General
Public License, version 3 or later; see COPYING.  The full manual is in
GREP.TXT (plain text) and grep.1 (man page source).

Setting up
----------
The kit installs GREP$STARTUP.COM into SYS$STARTUP and runs it once.  To
define GREP$ROOT at every boot, the system manager adds this line to
SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM:

    $ @SYS$STARTUP:GREP$STARTUP.COM

Users then define the commands in LOGIN.COM:

    $ @GREP$ROOT:[000000]GREP$SETUP.COM

That defines grep, and egrep and fgrep as "grep -E" and "grep -F".

Things that differ from Unix
----------------------------
- Case of options and patterns.  With the default PARSE_STYLE=TRADITIONAL,
  DCL upper-cases an unquoted command line and the C run-time library then
  lower-cases it, so "grep -E Foo" reaches grep as "grep -e foo".  Either
      $ SET PROCESS/PARSE_STYLE=EXTENDED
  (grep then keeps the case as typed), or put such arguments in quotes:
      $ grep "-E" "Foo|Bar" file.txt

- Exit status.  grep exits with 0 (a line matched), 1 (no line matched) or
  2 (an error).  DCL sees these as C-facility condition values; the code is
      $ code = ($STATUS .AND. %X7F8) / 8

- File names are reported in Unix form (dir/file.txt).  -r and -R search
  directory trees.

- Locales.  Choose one with a logical name or environment variable, e.g.
      $ DEFINE LC_ALL "UTF8-20"
  VMS has no "en_US.UTF-8"; use the generic UTF8-xx locales (see
  SYS$I18N_LOCALE:).  IA64 ships only UTF8-20, which covers characters of
  up to three bytes.

- grep -P (Perl regular expressions) is not available in this release.

- grep sets several C run-time features at start-up (Unix file names, case
  preservation, ...).  Defining the corresponding DECC$ logical name
  yourself overrides grep's choice.
